becomes:

```text
state == waiting_for_dest
        ↓
LOCATION → destination
```

No ambiguity remains.

---

# The generator therefore needs two layers

This is the important part.

### Layer 1 — semantic template

Internally:

```python
Template(
    text="{request} من {pickup} ل{dest}",
    entities=[
        ("pickup", "{pickup}"),
        ("dest", "{dest}")
    ]
)
```

### Layer 2 — NER output

The generator converts both placeholders to:

```text
B-LOCATION/I-LOCATION
```

while retaining metadata internally during generation that tells us:

```text
location_1 = pickup
location_2 = destination
```

We don't necessarily have to put that metadata into the final `master.jsonl`; the template generator simply needs it while constructing the example.

---

# One caveat: prepositions aren't the only signal

Consider:

> بدي تكسي من كافيه راية ومشفى الحكمة

That's potentially ambiguous.

But:

> بدي تكسي من كافيه راية لمشفى الحكمة

is very clear.

So our generator should deliberately generate **both strong and natural variations**, but we shouldn't generate grammatically dubious combinations just to increase dataset size.

For example:

```text
من {PICKUP} ل{DEST}
من {PICKUP} لعند {DEST}
من {PICKUP} إلى {DEST}
من {PICKUP} ع{DEST}
```

are useful.

And:

```text
{PICKUP} {DEST}
```

is **not** useful for a complete request because the model has no reliable textual relationship to learn.

---

## So I would change the previous recommendation

I recommend:

```text
NER entities:
    LOCATION
    TIME
```

rather than:

```text
NER entities:
    PICKUP
    DEST
    TIME
```

and make the pipeline:

```text
User message
     │
     ├── Intent classifier
     │
     └── NER
          │
          └── LOCATION spans
                  │
                  ▼
          Context interpreter
                  │
          ┌───────┴────────┐
          │                │
     explicit syntax    session state
          │                │
          └───────┬────────┘
                  ▼
             PICKUP / DEST
                  │
                  ▼
            State machine
```

This also means your dataset templates **do handle both pickup and destination**; we just annotate both as `LOCATION` and let the template's structure teach the downstream interpreter their roles.

I would make this change before we generate the dataset, because otherwise we'll generate thousands of examples using the old `PICKUP/DEST` scheme and then have to regenerate them.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Yes. The **destination-first family still applies**, and it is actually an important reason to separate `LOCATION` from `PICKUP/DEST`.

For example, we had:

```text
{REQUEST} ل{DEST} من {PICKUP}
{REQUEST} لعند {DEST} من {PICKUP}
{REQUEST} إلى {DEST} من {PICKUP}
{REQUEST} ع{DEST} من {PICKUP}
```

So:

> بدي تكسي لمشفى الحكمة من كافيه راية

would be annotated:

```json
{
  "text": "بدي تكسي لمشفى الحكمة من كافيه راية",
  "intents": ["request_taxi"],
  "tokens": ["بدي", "تكسي", "لمشفى", "الحكمة", "من", "كافيه", "راية"],
  "ner_tags": ["O", "O", "O", "B-LOCATION", "I-LOCATION", "O", "B-LOCATION", "I-LOCATION"]
}
```

The **first LOCATION is DEST**, despite appearing first.

The interpreter gets:

```text
لمشفى الحكمة ← destination
من كافيه راية ← pickup
```

So the rule is **not**:

> first LOCATION = pickup, second LOCATION = destination.

It is:

> **The syntactic construction surrounding each LOCATION determines its role.**

That means our templates should retain explicit semantic placeholders internally:

```text
{REQUEST} من {PICKUP} ل{DEST}
{REQUEST} من {PICKUP} لعند {DEST}

{REQUEST} ل{DEST} من {PICKUP}
{REQUEST} لعند {DEST} من {PICKUP}
```

and the generator converts both placeholders to `LOCATION` in the actual NER output.

This is also why we should **not discard the template metadata during generation**. Internally, the generator knows:

```text
LOCATION span #1 → DEST
LOCATION span #2 → PICKUP
```

even though the NER tags are simply:

```text
LOCATION
LOCATION
```

The eventual state-aware interpreter can then use the linguistic cues:

```text
من X       → pickup
لـ X       → destination
لعند X     → destination
إلى X      → destination
عـ X       → destination
```

while bare locations are resolved from state:

```text
waiting_for_pickup → LOCATION = pickup
waiting_for_dest   → LOCATION = destination
```

So **yes, all the destination-first templates remain in the generator**. We should preserve both ordering families rather than simplifying them away.
